WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.082
Semidefinite programming is a powerful tool for
combinatorial optimization,

00:00:05.162 --> 00:00:09.620
but it has long been considered too expensive to
run at real-world scale.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.910
This paper introduces USBS, Unified Spectral
Bundling with Sketching,

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:18.945
a provably correct solver that is fast, scales to
billions of decision variables,

00:00:19.025 --> 00:00:22.799
and, crucially, can reuse a previous solution as a
warm start.

00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:26.094
On a MaxCut instance with over two billion variables,

00:00:26.174 --> 00:00:30.943
USBS delivers a five-hundred-times speedup over the
previous state of the art,

00:00:31.023 --> 00:00:36.040
and warm-starting alone can accelerate convergence
by more than one hundred times.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.702
Semidefinite programs can model an enormous range
of practical problems,

00:00:39.782 --> 00:00:43.063
from combinatorial optimization to neural network
verification and control.

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:46.425
But solving them at scale is hard.

00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:49.786
The classic approaches require projecting onto the
semidefinite cone,

00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:53.148
which needs a full eigendecomposition that scales
cubically with problem size.

00:00:53.228 --> 00:00:56.509
Recent sketching methods, like CGAL,

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:59.871
avoid storing the full matrix and scale much further,

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:03.232
but they pay for it: as the problem grows they need
more and more iterations,

00:01:03.312 --> 00:01:06.594
so convergence slows down.

00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:09.955
Worse,

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:13.317
they rely on iteration-dependent parameter
schedules that prevent them from reliably reusing

00:01:13.397 --> 00:01:16.678
a previous solution as a warm start,

00:01:16.758 --> 00:01:20.040
which is exactly what many real applications need.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.810
In practice, you rarely solve a single SDP in isolation.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.483
Data arrives incrementally,

00:01:25.563 --> 00:01:31.555
or you solve a sequence of tightly-related
subproblems inside a mixed-integer or interactive loop.

00:01:31.635 --> 00:01:36.017
In all these settings, each new problem is nearly
identical to the last,

00:01:36.097 --> 00:01:41.098
so being able to warm-start from the previous
solution should give a huge speedup.

00:01:41.178 --> 00:01:46.613
Spectral bundle methods are an appealing framework
here: they have low per-iteration cost

00:01:46.693 --> 00:01:48.534
and fast empirical convergence.

00:01:48.614 --> 00:01:55.288
But previous spectral bundle methods handled either
only equality constraints or only inequality constraints,

00:01:55.368 --> 00:02:01.360
and none had an efficient standalone implementation
that could be evaluated on truly massive SDPs.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.289
This paper presents USBS, a unified spectral bundle
method with sketching.

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:08.918
It makes several contributions.

00:02:08.998 --> 00:02:12.548
First, it handles general SDPs with both equality
and inequality constraints,

00:02:12.628 --> 00:02:16.177
unlike prior spectral bundle methods.

00:02:16.257 --> 00:02:19.806
Second,

00:02:19.886 --> 00:02:23.435
it can be augmented with an optional matrix
sketching technique that dramatically improves

00:02:23.515 --> 00:02:27.065
scalability while keeping convergence fast.

00:02:27.145 --> 00:02:30.694
Third, it reliably leverages warm-start initializations.

00:02:30.774 --> 00:02:34.323
The method comes with provable non-asymptotic
convergence guarantees,

00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:37.952
and it exposes parameters that let the user trade
off per-iteration cost against convergence speed.

00:02:38.032 --> 00:02:41.582
Finally,

00:02:41.662 --> 00:02:45.211
the authors release a standalone implementation in
pure JAX that runs efficiently on CPUs,

00:02:45.291 --> 00:02:48.840
GPUs, and TPUs.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.544
Here is how USBS works.

00:02:50.624 --> 00:02:56.464
It starts from the dual semidefinite program and
rewrites it as a single unconstrained objective,

00:02:56.544 --> 00:03:01.774
a penalized dual that combines the largest
eigenvalue of C minus A-star y with a linear

00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:05.008
term and an indicator for the inequality constraints.

00:03:05.088 --> 00:03:09.464
This penalized objective is then minimized with a
proximal bundle method.

00:03:09.544 --> 00:03:12.454
At each iteration, instead of the full objective,

00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:17.703
USBS builds a cheap lower model over a
low-dimensional subspace spanned by the current

00:03:17.783 --> 00:03:22.402
maximum eigenvectors and a few directions carrying
past spectral information.

00:03:22.482 --> 00:03:27.102
It proposes a candidate iterate by taking a
proximal step against this model,

00:03:27.182 --> 00:03:29.055
solving a small minimax problem.

00:03:29.135 --> 00:03:34.487
If the true objective decreases by at least a fixed
fraction of what the model predicted,

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:36.684
it accepts the step, a descent step;

00:03:36.764 --> 00:03:40.285
otherwise it takes a null step but still refines
the model.

00:03:40.365 --> 00:03:43.764
Because only a small set of eigenvectors and, optionally,

00:03:43.844 --> 00:03:47.120
a low-rank sketch of the primal matrix are ever stored,

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.050
the whole procedure scales to enormous problems.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.615
The method is tested across three very different
application areas.

00:03:54.695 --> 00:03:55.888
The first is MaxCut,

00:03:55.968 --> 00:04:03.336
using ten graphs from the DIMACS10 collection
ranging from sixteen thousand up to three point seven million vertices,

00:04:03.416 --> 00:04:07.919
the largest yielding more than ten to the
thirteenth decision variables.

00:04:07.999 --> 00:04:10.911
The second is the quadratic assignment problem,

00:04:10.991 --> 00:04:13.521
a notoriously hard combinatorial problem,

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:18.677
using instances from QAPLIB and TSPLIB with sizes
up to one hundred ninety-eight,

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:22.942
whose SDP relaxation has on the order of
n-to-the-fourth variables,

00:04:23.022 --> 00:04:24.979
reaching one point five billion.

00:04:25.059 --> 00:04:29.626
The third is interactive entity resolution using
existential constraints,

00:04:29.706 --> 00:04:33.954
on three author-coreference datasets: PubMed, QIAN,
and SCAD-zbMATH.

00:04:34.034 --> 00:04:38.792
In each case, warm starts are constructed naturally
from a slightly smaller,

00:04:38.872 --> 00:04:40.320
closely related problem.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.398
The results are striking.

00:04:44.478 --> 00:04:48.177
On MaxCut,

00:04:48.257 --> 00:04:51.955
USBS reaches an accurate solution on all ten
instances in twenty-eight hours or less,

00:04:52.035 --> 00:04:55.733
even without a warm start, while the previous
state-of-the-art solver, CGAL,

00:04:55.813 --> 00:04:59.512
fails to reach an accurate solution on seven of the
ten instances within seventy-two hours.

00:04:59.592 --> 00:05:03.290
On an instance with over two billion decision variables,

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:07.068
USBS is five hundred times faster than CGAL.

00:05:07.148 --> 00:05:10.847
On the quadratic assignment and entity-resolution tasks,

00:05:10.927 --> 00:05:14.625
USBS reaches better relative gaps and lower
cumulative solve times,

00:05:14.705 --> 00:05:18.403
and the gap in its favor grows as the problems get larger.

00:05:18.483 --> 00:05:22.182
Across all three settings, USBS reliably takes
advantage of warm starts,

00:05:22.262 --> 00:05:25.960
whereas CGAL often cannot.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.990
Two ablations are especially informative.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.053
First, warm-starting: for USBS,

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:36.979
initializing from the previous solution can speed
up convergence by more than one hundred

00:05:37.059 --> 00:05:39.242
times compared with cold-starting,

00:05:39.322 --> 00:05:44.568
and, importantly, USBS actually realizes this
benefit while CGAL usually cannot.

00:05:44.648 --> 00:05:50.161
Second, the model parameters: the number of current
eigenvectors, kc, matters a lot,

00:05:50.241 --> 00:05:53.689
and larger values give better convergence in general.

00:05:53.769 --> 00:05:58.949
This contrasts with the original spectral bundle
method, which fixed kc to one.

00:05:59.029 --> 00:06:01.612
The number of past spectral vectors, kp,

00:06:01.692 --> 00:06:06.538
turns out to be much less helpful and can sometimes
even harm convergence,

00:06:06.618 --> 00:06:10.200
so the recommended settings keep kc large and kp small.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:15.711
To summarize the numbers that matter most: a
five-hundred-times speedup over the previous

00:06:15.791 --> 00:06:20.161
state of the art on a problem with more than two
billion decision variables;

00:06:20.241 --> 00:06:24.494
more than a one-hundred-times convergence speedup
just from warm-starting;

00:06:24.574 --> 00:06:29.003
ten out of ten MaxCut instances solved by USBS in
twenty-eight hours or less,

00:06:29.083 --> 00:06:32.926
versus only three out of ten for CGAL even given
seventy-two hours.

00:06:33.006 --> 00:06:37.903
USBS scales to problems with over
ten-to-the-thirteenth decision variables and solves

00:06:37.983 --> 00:06:42.060
quadratic-assignment relaxations with one point
five billion variables.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:43.819
The takeaway is simple.

00:06:43.899 --> 00:06:49.594
USBS shows that large-scale semidefinite
programming does not have to be slow or restricted

00:06:49.674 --> 00:06:51.497
to a narrow class of problems.

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:56.764
By unifying equality and inequality constraints in
a single spectral bundle method,

00:06:56.844 --> 00:07:01.967
adding optional sketching for scalability, and
making warm-starting actually work,

00:07:02.047 --> 00:07:07.043
it turns SDPs that were previously considered
intractable into a practical tool,

00:07:07.123 --> 00:07:10.850
complete with an open, hardware-flexible JAX
implementation.
